[2026-11-27T21:11:08.276Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_172","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'cancel|warning|Fast|main.*queue' dev/log/issues/207/pulls/208/templates/CI-CD-BEST-PRACTICES.md | head -55; for lang in rust js csharp python java cpp; do echo \\\"\"'$lang\"; jq -r .sha dev/log/issues/207/pulls/208/templates/$lang-tree.json; rg -n '\"'\"'^(  test:|  push:|      group: .*github.ref.*test|      cancel-in-progress: true)'\"' /tmp/link-cli-207-templates/\"'$lang/.github/workflows/release.yml | head -15; done'","aggregated_output":"130:  if: ${{ !cancelled() && needs.plan.outputs.test-db != '[]' && (github.event_name != 'pull_request' || github.event.action == 'ready_for_review') }}\n206:| C#                    | .NET analyzers (warnings as errors)                 |\n208:| C/C++                 | clang-tidy + cppcheck (warnings as errors)          |\n210:| Swift                 | Compiler warnings as errors (`-warnings-as-errors`) |\n213:### 5. Fast-Fail Job Ordering\n218:Fast checks (~7-30s each):     Slow checks (~1-10 min each):\n232:    !cancelled() &&\n336:**Separate cancellable read-only checks from non-cancellable write jobs.** Configure concurrency at the job level when a workflow contains both kinds of work:\n345:      cancel-in-progress: ${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' && github.event.action == 'synchronize' }}\n350:    if: ${{ !cancelled() && needs.lint.result == 'success' }}\n355:      cancel-in-progress: false\n360:- **Dependent writers:** Use `needs` and require successful prerequisites. A cancelled prerequisite must make its write job not start.\n361:- **Active writers:** Give every release, deploy, tag, generated-content push, and other write job the same repository-scoped group with `cancel-in-progress: false`. An already started writer finishes while the next writer waits in the queue, including writers from another workflow file.\n362:- **Workflow scope:** Allow workflow-level cancellation only for events that cannot publish, deploy, or change repository state. An active writer would also be interrupted if its workflow were cancelled.\n364:By default, a concurrency group keeps at most one running and one pending job; a newer pending writer replaces the older pending writer. If every queued write must run, add `queue: max` to the writer's concurrency block (up to 100 jobs can wait). `queue: max` cannot be combined with `cancel-in-progress: true`, and execution order follows when jobs start waiting rather than workflow dispatch order, so write jobs should remain idempotent. See [GitHub's concurrency documentation](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/write-workflows/choose-when-workflows-run/control-workflow-concurrency) for the current queue limits and semantics.\n366:Use `!cancelled()` instead of `always()` in job conditions so cancellation propagates correctly through the job graph. A bare `always()` can keep downstream work running after cancellation.\n387:  if (!isNonFastForward(result) || attempt === maxAttempts) throw new CommandFailedError('git', ['push', remote, branch], result);\n399:Group commit checks by workflow and PR and cancel only superseded `synchronize` runs. Give ready and manual runs a per-run key so GitHub also preserves queued runs. Group pushes by SHA so each main revision keeps its checks.\n404:  cancel-in-progress: ${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' && github.event.action == 'synchronize' }}\n407:Apply this grouping to each cancellable job if the workflow uses job-level groups, keeping the job and any matrix identity in its key. Keep the shared non-cancelling group for publishing and deployment jobs. Workflow-level cancellation is safe only when every event eligible for cancellation runs checks without publishing or deploying. See the [language template patches](../examples/ci-cd-pull-request-tiers/README.md).\n503:**A pull request exists to test the code; a push to the default branch exists to produce a release.** Those are different jobs, and a missing credential means different things to each. On a pull request it is a warning — forks have no secrets, and the code can still be tested. On the default branch it is the answer: if any credential needed for any planned release is unusable, nothing the run does afterwards can produce a release, and every minute spent building is waste.\n521:  if: ${{ !cancelled() && needs.release-preflight.result == 'success' }}\n524:- **Probe with a write, not with a login.** ghcr.io's token endpoint returns 200 for any scope and verifies nothing — the push then fails 403. docker.io answers an anonymous push-scope request with 200 and a pull-only `access` claim. Open a blob upload session (`POST /v2/<repo>/blobs/uploads/`) and cancel it with `DELETE <Location>`: one round trip, nothing stored, and the only form of the check that is not a guess.\nrust\n1d2…121\n4:  push:\n68:      cancel-in-progress: true\n81:      cancel-in-progress: true\n124:      cancel-in-progress: true\n185:      cancel-in-progress: true\n220:      cancel-in-progress: true\n256:      cancel-in-progress: true\n299:      cancel-in-progress: true\n355:      cancel-in-progress: true\n402:      cancel-in-progress: true\n441:      cancel-in-progress: true\n541:  test:\n548:      group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}-test-${{ matrix.os }}-${{ matrix.toolchain }}\n549:      cancel-in-progress: true\n649:      group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}-script-tests\njs\n8e7…989\n4:  push:\n65:      cancel-in-progress: true\n97:      group: check-${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}-test-compilation\n98:      cancel-in-progress: true\n124:      cancel-in-progress: true\n158:      cancel-in-progress: true\n184:      cancel-in-progress: true\n229:      cancel-in-progress: true\n293:  test:\n304:      group: check-${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}-test-${{ matrix.runtime }}-${{ matrix.os }}\n305:      cancel-in-progress: true\n418:      cancel-in-progress