[2026-11-27T21:30:17.053Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_427","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,155p' .github/workflows/javascript-stage.yml\"","aggregated_output":"name: JavaScript stage\n\n# The JavaScript stage of the stage gate (issue #111): ESLint and Prettier,\n# every Node unit test (`npm run test:js`) and the cross-language parity\n# checker. It needs no Rust, wasm-pack or .NET, so it finishes in a few\n# minutes.\n#\n# Every workflow that runs Rust or C# calls this file as its first job\n# (`javascript: uses: ./.github/workflows/javascript-stage.yml`) and lists\n# `javascript` in the `needs:` of each Rust or C# job, so those jobs start\n# only after this stage succeeded for the same commit. A failed, cancelled or\n# skipped stage leaves them skipped. .github/scripts/workflow-policy.test.mjs\n# enforces that wiring. javascript.yml runs the same stage on its own for\n# every pull request, with no path filter, behind the required\n# \"Pipeline Status (javascript)\" check.\n\non:\n  workflow_call:\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\n# Provide Git config to actions/checkout itself; checkout runs git init before\n# any workflow step can configure Git, which otherwise prints the\n# default-branch hint into every log.\nenv:\n  GIT_CONFIG_COUNT: '1'\n  GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0: ini…nch\n  GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0: main\n\njobs:\n  stage:\n    name: JavaScript stage\n    runs-on: ubuntu-24.04\n    timeout-minutes: 15\n    # In a called workflow, github.workflow is the caller's name, so each\n    # caller cancels only its own superseded stage.\n    concurrency:\n      group: check-${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' && github.event.action == 'synchronize' && github.ref || github.event_name == 'push' && github.sha || github.run_id }}-javascript-stage\n      cancel-in-progress: ${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' && github.event.action == 'synchronize' }}\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v7\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n          # The parity checker compares the manifest with the base commit.\n          fetch-depth: 0\n\n      - name: Setup Node.js\n        uses: actions/setup-node@v7\n        with:\n          node-version: '26.x'\n          cache: npm\n          cache-dependency-path: js/package-lock.json\n\n      - name: Install npm dependencies\n        working-directory: js\n        run: npm ci\n\n      - name: Lint and format check\n        working-directory: js\n        run: npm run lint\n\n      # js/test, csharp/scripts, .github/scripts and parity unit tests. Until\n      # issue #111 these ran only inside wasm.yml, after a Rust toolchain and\n      # wasm-pack install.\n      # js/test/golden-fixtures.test.mjs writes the receipt of the fixtures\n      # it loaded; the next step proves none was skipped (docs/PARITY.md).\n      - name: Test JavaScript\n        working-directory: js\n        env:\n          GOLDEN_FIXTURES_RECEIPT: ${{ runner.temp }}/golden-javascript.json\n        run: npm run test:js\n\n      - name: Check that the JavaScript suite loaded every golden fixture\n        env:\n          RECEIPT: ${{ runner.temp }}/golden-javascript.json\n        run: node parity/check-parity.mjs --receipt javascript \"$RECEIPT\"\n\n      # Fails on a one-sided feature change, a stale fingerprint, an\n      # unassigned source file or a drifted golden fixture (docs/PARITY.md).\n      # Pull requests compare with their base; pushes with the previous tip.\n      - name: Check cross-language parity\n        env:\n          PARITY_BASE_SHA: ${{ github.event.pull_request.base.sha || github.event.before }}\n          PARITY_DEBUG: ${{ runner.debug }}\n        run: node parity/check-parity.mjs\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
