[2026-11-27T23:19:40.206Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1130","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/github/docs/main/content/code-security/how-tos/manage-security-alerts/manage-code-scanning-alerts/resolve-alerts.md -o dev/log/issues/207/pulls/208/ci-logs/reviews/github-resolving-code-scanning-alerts.md && rg -n -A46 '\"'^## Removing stale configurations'\"' dev/log/issues/207/pulls/208/ci-logs/reviews/github-resolving-code-scanning-alerts.md\"","aggregated_output":"162:## Removing stale configurations and alerts from a branch\n163-\n164-You may have multiple {% data variables.product.prodname_code_scanning %} configurations on a single repository. When run, multiple configurations can generate the same alert. Additionally, if the configurations are run on different schedules, the alert statuses may become out-of-date for infrequent or stale configurations. For more information on alerts from multiple configurations, see [AUTOTITLE](/code-security/concepts/code-scanning/code-scanning-alerts#about-alerts-from-multiple-configurations).\n165-\n166-{% data reusables.repositories.navigate-to-repo %}\n167-{% data reusables.repositories.sidebar-security %}\n168-{% data reusables.repositories.sidebar-code-scanning-alerts %}\n169-1. Under \"{% data variables.product.prodname_code_scanning_caps %}\", click a {% data variables.product.prodname_code_scanning %} alert.\n170-1. In the \"Affected branches\" section of the sidebar, click the desired branch.\n171-1. In the \"Configurations analyzing\" dialog, review details of the configurations that reported this alert on the selected branch. To delete an unwanted configuration for the desired branch, click {% octicon \"trash\" aria-label=\"Delete configuration\" %}.\n172-\n173-   If you delete a configuration by mistake, click **Cancel** to avoid applying your changes.\n174-\n175-   ![Screenshot of the \"Configurations analyzing\" modal. The \"Delete configuration\" icon is outlined in dark orange.](/assets/images/help/repository/code-scanning-remove-configuration.png)\n176-\n177-1. Once you have removed any unwanted configurations and confirmed the expected configurations are displayed, click **Save changes**.\n178-\n179-   If you save your changes after accidentally deleting a configuration, re-run the configuration to update the alert. For more information on re-running configurations that use {% data variables.product.prodname_actions %}, see [AUTOTITLE](/actions/how-tos/manage-workflow-runs/re-run-workflows-and-jobs#re-running-all-the-jobs-in-a-workflow).\n180-\n181-> [!NOTE]\n182-> * If you remove all {% data variables.product.prodname_code_scanning %} configurations for the default branch of your repository, the default branch will remain in the \"Affected branches\" sidebar, but it will not be analyzed by any configurations.\n183-> * If you remove all {% data variables.product.prodname_code_scanning %} configurations for any branch other than the default branch of your repository, that branch will be removed from the \"Affected branches\" sidebar.\n184-\n185-## Further reading\n186-\n187-* [AUTOTITLE](/code-security/how-tos/manage-security-alerts/manage-code-scanning-alerts/triage-alerts-in-pull-requests)\n188-* [AUTOTITLE](/code-security/how-tos/find-and-fix-code-vulnerabilities/configure-code-scanning/configure-code-scanning)\n189-* [AUTOTITLE](/code-security/concepts/code-scanning/integration-with-code-scanning)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
